course: myths
lesson: 23
title: Petrol stations, pools and things that go bang
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Dad snaps "Phone off!" at the petrol pump, your big cousin swears the pool water turns red if you wee, and Ella says a shark once swam into the hotel pool through the pipes.
explain: Safety myths are tricky because some are made up, some are true, and some give good advice for the wrong reason; laughing at all of them could get someone hurt.
explain: Gran's "never mix bleach with other cleaners" is completely true: some mixtures give off poisonous gas. "Get out of the pool when there's thunder" is true too.
explain: But a dropped penny can't kill, there's no magic dye for wee, and no shark can squeeze through pool pipes.
explain: The smart move is to sort them: "Is it true? Is the advice still good? What would really happen?", and follow safety signs even when the reason on the sign is a bit off.
fact: There is no confirmed case of a mobile phone setting petrol alight at a pump; most fires at pumps start from static electricity (the charge that builds up on you and gives a little spark or zap), for example when someone gets back in the car and then touches the nozzle.
fact: Petrol stations still ask people not to use phones at the pump, and you should follow their rules; a phone also distracts people near fuel and cars.
fact: No chemical exists that turns pool water red around someone who wees; the story is told to stop children doing it, and weeing in a pool is still unclean and unfair on other swimmers.
fact: A shark could not get into a hotel pool through the pipes, which are narrow, have filters, and carry chlorinated fresh water (cleaned with a chemical called chlorine), not sea water.
fact: A penny dropped from a skyscraper slows down in the air and tumbles; it would sting but would not kill, though dropping heavier things from a height can be deadly.
fact: Being in water during a thunderstorm is dangerous, so you should get out of an outdoor pool at the first thunder and wait until the storm has passed.
fact: Static shocks from a trampoline can make your hair stand up and give a little zap, but they are harmless.
fact: Never mix bleach with other cleaners: some mixtures make poisonous gases.
fact: Mints dropped into cola make a fizzy fountain and a sticky mess, not an explosion; a bottle with its lid screwed on is different and should never be used.
fact: A phone charger in bed will not electrocute you (give you a deadly electric shock), but a phone or charger under a pillow or duvet can overheat and start a fire, so charge it on a hard surface.
fact: Holding your breath does help you float, because air in your lungs makes you lighter for your size.
fact: A safety rule can be good advice even when the scary reason told with it is wrong.
exercise: What causes most fires at petrol pumps? | Static electricity, such as touching the nozzle after getting back in the car | Mobile phones ringing near the pump | Hot sunshine shining on the petrol | Children talking loudly on the forecourt
exercise: Should you follow a petrol station's "no phones" sign? | Yes, follow their rules, and phones distract people near fuel | No, the sign is wrong, so you can safely ignore it and carry on | Only if your phone is a very old one | Only if it is raining at the pump
exercise: Is there a chemical that turns pool water red if you wee? | No, the story is told to stop children doing it | Yes, every public pool has to put it in the water by law | Yes, but it only works in outdoor pools in summer | Yes, it turns the water bright green, not red as people say
exercise: Why can't a shark get into a hotel pool through the pipes? | The pipes are narrow and filtered and carry fresh chlorinated water | Sharks are afraid of hotels and stay away | The pipes are always switched off at night | Sharks can only swim in warm sea water near beaches
exercise: What would a penny dropped from a skyscraper do to someone below? | Sting, but not kill, because air slows it down | Go straight through them like a bullet from a gun | Make a big hole in the pavement | Melt from the heat before it reached them
exercise: What should you do in an outdoor pool at the first thunder? | Get out and wait until the storm has passed | Stay in, because water protects you from lightning | Swim to the deep end where it is safer | Duck under the water until the thunder stops
exercise: Are static shocks from a trampoline dangerous? | No, they are harmless little zaps | Yes, they can stop your heart | Yes, but only on trampolines with nets | Yes, they can set the trampoline on fire
exercise: Why must you never mix bleach with other cleaners? | Some mixtures make poisonous gases | The bottles might crack in the cupboard | The cleaners stop smelling nice | The bleach loses its colour
exercise: What happens when mints are dropped into an open bottle of cola? | A fizzy fountain and a sticky mess | The kitchen blows up | The cola freezes solid at once | Nothing at all happens
exercise: What is the real danger of charging a phone under your pillow? | It can overheat and start a fire | It will electrocute you in your sleep | It gives you nightmares through the screen | It makes the phone forget its photos
exercise: Where is the safest place to charge a phone overnight? | On a hard surface, not under bedding | Under your pillow, so it stays nice and warm | Inside your duvet cover, close to your face | Under the mattress, so it is hidden from parents
exercise: Does holding your breath help you float? | Yes, air in your lungs makes you lighter for your size | No, it makes you sink like a stone | No, breathing has nothing whatever to do with floating in water | Only if you also close your eyes
exercise: Gran says never mix bleach with other cleaners. Is she right? | Yes, it is true and important | No, it is an old wives' tale | Only if the cleaners are blue | Only if you mix them in the bath
exercise: Can a safety rule be good even if the reason told with it is wrong? | Yes, like not using phones at the pump | No, if the reason is wrong the rule is wrong | Only if the rule is written on a sign | Only if a police officer tells you
test: "dad turned off his phone at the pump lol does it rly blow up" What's the honest answer? | No proven case, but follow the station's rules; static is the bigger risk | Yes, phones blow up petrol stations every week | Only iPhones do it, not other phones | No, so you can use your phone however you like
test: Your big cousin says the pool will go red if you wee. Is that why you shouldn't? | No dye exists, but weeing in a pool is still unfair and unclean | Yes, the red dye is the only reason not to | It's fine to wee in the pool, because the red dye doesn't actually exist | The dye exists but only works at night
test: Ella swears a shark came into the hotel pool through the pipes. Which question shows the story can't work? | How would a shark fit through narrow, filtered pipes? | What colour was the shark when it arrived in the pool? | Was it the deep end or the shallow end? | Did the hotel give the shark a room?
test: "if u drop a penny off the shard it kills someone, fact" What would really happen? | Air slows it; it would sting, but heavy things are still dangerous | It would kill instantly, so pennies are banned | It would bounce back up to the top | It would burn up in the air
test: The sky rumbles while you're in the outdoor pool, and Mia says lightning will hit the pool. What do you do? | Get out now; being in water in a storm is dangerous | Ignore her, it's only a myth | Stay in but keep your head down | Swim faster and faster so the lightning can't catch you
test: Callum's hair stands up on the trampoline and he shouts that the static could kill him. Is he right? | No, it's a harmless zap | Yes, he should call an ambulance | Yes, if he jumps for more than a minute | No, but it gives you a fever
test: A friend wants to mix two cleaners to make "super cleaner". What should you do? | Say no and tell an adult; some mixtures make poisonous gas | Mix them outside where it's safe | Mix them, but only a little | Add water so it isn't dangerous
test: "mentos + coke = kitchen explosion, my mate's kitchen got destroyed" What would really happen in an open bottle? | A fizzy fountain and a sticky mess | A blast that knocks down the walls | The bottle would fly to the ceiling | The cola would catch fire
test: Henry charges his phone under the duvet every night. What is the real risk? | It could overheat and start a fire | He will be electrocuted in his sleep | His phone will stop working forever | Nothing at all could ever happen
test: Ava says holding your breath helps you float. Her brother says that's a myth. Who is right? | Ava: air in the lungs helps you float | Her brother: breath makes no difference | Neither: holding your breath makes you sink | Both: it helps only in salty sea water
exam: Identify the principal cause of fires occurring at fuel pumps, as ascertained from investigations. | The discharge of static electricity | The operation of mobile telephones | The heating of fuel by sunlight | The use of electric vehicle chargers nearby
exam: It is given that thunder is audible while a person is swimming in an outdoor pool. Select the response consistent with established safety guidance. | The person should leave the water and remain out until the storm has passed | The person should remain submerged until the thunder ceases | The person should proceed to the deepest part of the pool | The person may continue swimming, the water affording protection
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the hazard of combining bleach with other cleaning products? | Certain combinations liberate poisonous gases | All such combinations are entirely harmless | The combination merely diminishes the bleach's colour | The combination solely impairs the cleaning effect
exam: Determine the principal hazard associated with the charging of a telephone beneath bedding. | Overheating, with a consequent risk of fire | Electrocution of the person sleeping beside it | Permanent loss of all the data stored upon it | Interference with the sleeper's dreams and rest
